<DocsLayout
  title="Workflows"
  subtitle="Versioned YAML checks and service steps run against the exact project commit that triggered them."
  description="CADCAMFUN workflows: YAML schema, triggers, registered steps, dependency execution, outputs, and persisted run results."
  toc={toc}
>
  <section id="status" class="doc-section">
    <h2>What a run evaluates</h2>
<p>Workflow files are committed under <code>.cadcamfun/workflows/</code> with a <code>.yml</code> or <code>.yaml</code> extension. The sidecar loads them from a selected committed ref. Editing an uncommitted drawing or workflow does not change the files seen by an existing run.</p>
    <p><code>@cadcamfun/workflow</code> parses the YAML, validates dependencies, matches triggers, and executes registered functions. It does not execute arbitrary shell commands, container images, or downloaded actions. Each <code>uses</code> identifier must exist in the runner's current registry before any step starts.</p>
  </section>

  <section id="package" class="doc-section">
    <h2>Write a workflow</h2>
<DocCode language="yaml" title="Validate every manually dispatched project">
{`name: Project checks
on:
  manual: true
jobs:
  validate:
    steps:
      - id: structure
        uses: project/validate
      - uses: project/require-gcode
      - uses: cam/sequence`}
    </DocCode>
    <p>A workflow has a name, <code>on</code> triggers, and jobs keyed by identifier. A job contains at least one step and can declare <code>needs</code>. A step has <code>uses</code>, optional <code>id</code> and name, optional <code>with</code> arguments, <code>if</code>, and <code>continueOnError</code>.</p>
    <p>Identifiers start with a letter and contain at most 64 letters, digits, underscores, or hyphens. Step identifiers are unique across the entire workflow. Parsing rejects invalid YAML, schema failures, unknown job dependencies, cyclic dependencies, and duplicate step identifiers.</p>
  </section>

  <section id="steps" class="doc-section">
    <h2>Built-in checks</h2>
<ul>
      <li><code>project/validate</code> parses committed JSON files and checks recognized drawing/component/toolpath entity IDs against their file names and verifies their names. It returns resource counts.</li>
      <li><code>project/require-gcode</code> fails if a committed toolpath has empty or missing G-code. It does not require that the project contain at least one toolpath.</li>
      <li><code>cam/verify</code> builds replay requests from committed toolpath G-code and <code>Toolpath.data.request</code>. It fails on error-severity replay findings, or also warnings when <code>with.severity</code> is <code>warning</code>. Optional arguments are <code>toolpath</code> and <code>tolerance</code>.</li>
      <li><code>cam/gcode</code> regenerates from the committed <code>data.request</code> and compares its trimmed NC with committed text. It accepts an optional <code>toolpath</code> selector.</li>
      <li><code>cam/sequence</code> checks stored requests for conflicting tool-number assignments and rest-machining requests whose preceding tool is not larger than the active tool.</li>
    </ul>
    <p>The CAM steps read the stored generator request format. Missing NC, missing generation requests, or unsupported verification requests are reported in <code>skipped</code> outputs where applicable; they do not automatically fail the step. Inspect the verified/matched count and skipped reasons. A green workflow containing only skipped CAM work is not proof of a verified program.</p>
  </section>

  <section id="step-ids" class="doc-section">
    <h2>Service-backed steps</h2>
<p>The live sidecar registers <code>vcs/commit</code>, <code>notify/push</code>, and, after the assistant service exists, <code>ai/prompt</code>. Registration is explicit: a runner created without these services fails a workflow naming an unavailable step.</p>
    <p><code>vcs/commit</code> requires a message and accepts a <code>files</code> record of text under <code>.cadcamfun/</code> with YAML, Markdown, JSON, or text extensions. It acts as the run's account and obeys protected-branch policy. The extra-file argument cannot directly replace CAD entity paths. The current implementation delegates to project commit without checking for a clean working copy: it also snapshots current database edits on the checked-out branch, which can differ from the ref being checked. Run this writing step only when that current branch and working state are intended to be committed.</p>
    <p><code>notify/push</code> creates an application notification for the run's actor, with optional title, body, and success/failure outcome. It is a persisted notification step; it does not by itself establish delivery through an external push provider.</p>
    <p><code>ai/prompt</code> requires a prompt and optionally accepts system text and a model. It can substitute committed file content and returns answer text and model identity. Missing credentials or a provider error fail this step; a model answer is advisory output, separate from deterministic geometry checks.</p>
    <p>The pure built-in steps are registered in <code>builtinSteps</code> (<code>apps/sidecar/src/services/workflow-steps.ts</code>): <code>project/validate</code>, <code>project/require-gcode</code>, <code>cam/verify</code>, <code>cam/gcode</code>, and <code>cam/sequence</code>. They need only the committed files and <code>@cadcamfun/core</code>, so they are available even when the assistant is not running.</p>
  </section>

  <section id="running" class="doc-section">
    <h2>Execution, outputs, and conditions</h2>
<p>Jobs run in dependency levels, with independent jobs in a level running concurrently. Steps within a job run sequentially. Jobs whose dependencies did not succeed are skipped. The default step condition is <code>success</code>; <code>always</code> runs after either result and <code>failure</code> runs after a prior unhandled step failure in that job.</p>
    <p><code>continueOnError: true</code> records a failing step event without setting the job's failure flag. Otherwise a failed step skips later success-only steps and marks the job failed. The overall result is success only when every job succeeds.</p>
    <p>Earlier named-step outputs can be referenced in <code>with</code> values using the expression shown below. A value consisting of exactly one expression retains its type; substitution inside longer strings converts the output to text. Jobs should declare dependencies when they consume another job's outputs.</p>
    <DocCode language="yaml" title="Use an earlier step output">
{`with:
  body: "Checked drawings: \${{ steps.structure.outputs.drawings }}"`}
    </DocCode>
  </section>

  <section id="on-disk" class="doc-section">
    <h2>Triggers and dispatch</h2>
<p><code>on.push</code> supports branch and changed-path glob filters; <code>on.pullRequest</code> filters the base branch; <code>on.manual</code> enables explicit dispatch. A single asterisk matches within a path segment and a double asterisk can span segments.</p>
    <p>The HTTP commit/push integration currently supplies an empty changed-path list to the automatic push trigger. Consequently a workflow with a push path filter does not match those events. Use branch-only push filters or manual dispatch for this integration until changed-path data is supplied.</p>
    <p>Read committed definitions at <code>GET /api/projects/:id/actions/workflows</code>, optionally with <code>ref</code>. Read one at <code>/workflows/:name</code>. A malformed file is returned with its content and a <code>problem</code> so it can be repaired. To edit a definition, include its safe workflow path in a project commit.</p>
    <p><code>POST /api/projects/:id/actions/dispatch</code> accepts an optional ref and dispatches every matching manual workflow at that ref; it is not a selector for one workflow name. Read saved results from <code>GET /api/projects/:id/actions/runs</code>.</p>
  </section>

  <section id="schema-exports" class="doc-section">
    <h2>Run persistence and events</h2>
<p>The runner records workflow, event, ref, commit SHA, status, timestamps, and accumulated events in <code>WorkflowRun</code>. Job events mark starts and ends; step events include index, identifier, status, optional outputs, and failure text. Invalid YAML is retained as a failed run with an invalid-file event.</p>
    <p>The current HTTP trigger path waits for workflows to settle and then publishes run-started and run-finished live events together. It does not stream each running step through that HTTP request. The core runner exposes an <code>onEvent</code> callback for integrations that need step progress.</p>
  </section>

  <section id="boundaries" class="doc-section">
    <h2>Interpret results precisely</h2>
<p>A workflow reports on its captured commit and registered step implementations. It does not certify later working-copy edits or remove an already recorded commit on failure. Inspect skipped checks, warning policy, and relevant toolpath evidence before using a successful run as a release gate.</p>
    <p>Implementation is split between <code>packages/workflow/src</code> for schema/trigger/execution and sidecar <code>workflow-runner.ts</code>, <code>workflow-steps.ts</code>, and <code>workflow-service-steps.ts</code> for committed project data, persisted runs, and application services.</p>
  </section>

  <section id="engine-example" class="doc-section">
    <h2>Run an isolated workflow engine example</h2>
<p>A package consumer supplies the registered functions and their typed context. This example parses a manual workflow, executes one registered check, and returns its job result without opening SQLite or invoking AI.</p>
    <DocCode language="ts" title="Parse, register, and run">
{String.raw`import { Effect } from "effect"
import { parseWorkflow, runWorkflow } from "@cadcamfun/workflow"

const source = [
  "name: Isolated check", "on:", "  manual: true", "jobs:",
  "  inspect:", "    steps:", "      - id: checked", "        uses: inspection/check"
].join("\n")
const result = await Effect.runPromise(Effect.gen(function* () {
  const workflow = yield* parseWorkflow(source)
  return yield* runWorkflow(workflow, {
    ctx: { drawingCount: 2 },
    registry: { "inspection/check": ({ ctx }) => Effect.succeed({ drawings: ctx.drawingCount }) }
  })
}))
console.log(result) // { status: "success", jobs: { inspect: "success" } }`}
    </DocCode>
    <p>Run within a workspace depending on Effect and <code>@cadcamfun/workflow</code>, such as <code>apps/sidecar</code>. The custom step above is local to the supplied registry; naming it in a desktop workflow does not install it into the production sidecar.</p>
    <p>Source: <code>packages/workflow/src/schema.ts</code> and <code>run.ts</code>.</p>
  </section>

  <section id="matching-and-outputs" class="doc-section">
    <h2>Match filters and consume outputs safely</h2>
<p>A push trigger requires its branch filter to match and, when a paths list exists, at least one supplied changed path to match. An omitted filter is unrestricted; an explicitly empty list matches nothing. A manual event requires <code>on.manual</code> to be present. Pull-request matching uses the base branch, rather than the head proposal name.</p>
    <p>Output substitution traverses nested arrays and records. An exact missing output expression evaluates to undefined; an absent output embedded in a longer string becomes empty text. Do not rely on a skipped or failed step to have produced an output. Declare <code>needs</code> for cross-job consumers so dependency scheduling establishes the data order.</p>
    <p>Step <code>if</code> is one of three literal modes, not a general expression language. Workflow variables, shell interpolation, matrix builds, arbitrary JavaScript conditions, and third-party action loading are not implemented by this engine. The separate <code>ai/prompt</code> handler substitutes committed <code>files.&lt;path&gt;</code> references in its prompt; ordinary steps do not receive that extra substitution.</p>
    <p>Source: <code>packages/workflow/src/trigger.ts</code>, <code>run.ts</code>, and <code>apps/sidecar/src/services/workflow-service-steps.ts</code>.</p>
    <p>An omitted branch or path filter matches every value. A manual dispatch matches only workflows that declare <code>on.manual</code>, and a pull-request event matches the workflow's <code>branches</code> globs against the pull request's base branch (<code>packages/workflow/src/trigger.ts</code>).</p>
  </section>

  <section id="troubleshooting" class="doc-section">
    <h2>Diagnose workflow results</h2>
<table>
      <thead><tr><th>Finding</th><th>Meaning and next check</th></tr></thead>
      <tbody>
        <tr><td>Workflow file is returned with <code>problem</code></td><td>Repair YAML/schema or dependency errors and commit the fixed file. File listing preserves invalid content for editing.</td></tr>
        <tr><td>Unknown step before execution</td><td>Compare every <code>uses</code> with registered sidecar step IDs. A package example's local registry is not the production registry.</td></tr>
        <tr><td>No run after push</td><td>Confirm the workflow exists in the committed ref, the branch matches, and no unsupported HTTP changed-path filter is excluding the event.</td></tr>
        <tr><td>Run is successful but <code>verified: 0</code></td><td>Read <code>skipped</code>. Missing generator requests or unsupported toolpaths can leave the check without geometric evidence.</td></tr>
        <tr><td>Dependent job skipped</td><td>Inspect the result of each <code>needs</code> job; dependency failure prevents the job from starting, including its always-conditioned steps.</td></tr>
        <tr><td>Run remains running after process termination</td><td>Inspect its timestamps/events and the exact commit. The runner persists start and final state separately and does not automatically resume an interrupted run.</td></tr>
      </tbody>
    </table>
    <p>Saved run history returns the latest 50 runs by default at the service boundary. Live event notifications are best-effort side channels: inspect persisted rows for the durable run result instead of treating notification delivery as the execution record.</p>
    <p>Source: <code>apps/sidecar/src/services/workflow-runner.ts</code>, <code>workflow-steps.ts</code>, and <code>http/vcs.ts</code>.</p>
  </section>

    <section id="file-format" class="doc-section">
    <h2>Workflow file format</h2>
    <p>A workflow is a YAML file at <code>.cadcamfun/workflows/&lt;name&gt;.yml</code> in the project repository, versioned with the model it checks. Its shape mirrors GitHub Actions (<code>on</code>, <code>jobs</code>, <code>needs</code>, <code>steps</code>, <code>uses</code>, <code>with</code>), but <code>uses</code> always names a registered CADCAMFUN step. A workflow cannot run a shell command or a container image.</p>
    <DocCode language="yaml" title=".cadcamfun/workflows/release-check.yml">
{"name: Release check\non:\n  push:\n    branches: [main, \u0022release/*\u0022]\n    paths: [\u0022toolpaths/**\u0022]\n  pullRequest:\n    branches: [main]\n  manual: {}\njobs:\n  validate:\n    steps:\n      - uses: project/validate\n      - uses: project/require-gcode\n  verify:\n    needs: [validate]\n    steps:\n      - id: verify\n        uses: cam/verify\n        with: { severity: warning }\n  gcode:\n    needs: [validate]\n    steps:\n      - uses: cam/gcode\n      - uses: cam/sequence\n  report:\n    needs: [verify, gcode]\n    steps:\n      - id: review\n        uses: ai/prompt\n        with:\n          prompt: \u0022Summarise risks in this program: ${{ files.toolpaths/op1.json }}\u0022\n        continueOnError: true\n      - uses: notify/push\n        if: always\n        with:\n          title: \u0022Release check finished\u0022\n          body: \u0022${{ steps.review.outputs.text }}\u0022"}
    </DocCode>
    <figure class="not-prose my-6">
      <svg viewBox="0 0 560 170" role="img" aria-label="Job graph: validate runs first; verify and gcode run concurrently after it; report runs last with if always" style="width:100%;max-width:600px;height:auto">
        <g fill="none" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="1.5">
          <rect x="10" y="65" width="110" height="40" rx="6"/><rect x="190" y="15" width="110" height="40" rx="6"/>
          <rect x="190" y="115" width="110" height="40" rx="6"/><rect x="380" y="65" width="150" height="40" rx="6"/>
          <path d="M120 85 L190 35 M120 85 L190 135 M300 35 L380 85 M300 135 L380 85"/>
        </g>
        <g fill="currentColor" font-family="monospace" font-size="12">
          <text x="30" y="90">validate</text><text x="215" y="40">verify</text><text x="218" y="140">gcode</text><text x="398" y="90">report (needs both)</text>
          <text x="10" y="20">level 0</text><text x="190" y="10">level 1 (concurrent)</text><text x="380" y="60">level 2</text>
        </g>
      </svg>
      <figcaption class="text-[13px]">Jobs run level by level. Jobs in the same level run concurrently, and a job runs only when every job in its <code>needs</code> succeeded; otherwise it is reported as <code>skipped</code>.</figcaption>
    </figure>
    <table>
      <thead><tr><th>Key</th><th>Rules</th></tr></thead>
      <tbody>
        <tr><td><code>name</code></td><td>Required, 1–128 characters.</td></tr>
        <tr><td><code>on.push</code></td><td>Optional <code>branches</code> and <code>paths</code> glob lists. A push matches when the branch matches and at least one changed path matches.</td></tr>
        <tr><td><code>on.pullRequest</code></td><td>Optional <code>branches</code> globs, matched against the pull request's base branch when it is opened.</td></tr>
        <tr><td><code>on.manual</code></td><td>Any value (for example <code>&#123;&#125;</code>) enables dispatch from the desktop or the mobile companion.</td></tr>
        <tr><td><code>jobs.&lt;id&gt;</code></td><td>Job IDs start with a letter and may contain letters, digits, <code>_</code>, and <code>-</code> (at most 64 characters). Each job needs at least one step.</td></tr>
        <tr><td><code>needs</code></td><td>Job IDs that must succeed first. Unknown IDs and cycles are rejected when the file is parsed.</td></tr>
        <tr><td><code>steps[].uses</code></td><td>A lowercase step identifier with at least one slash, such as <code>cam/verify</code>.</td></tr>
        <tr><td><code>steps[].id</code></td><td>Optional; unique across the whole workflow. Required for later steps to read its outputs.</td></tr>
        <tr><td><code>steps[].with</code></td><td>Free-form parameters for the step. Strings are interpolated.</td></tr>
        <tr><td><code>steps[].if</code></td><td><code>success</code> (default: run only if nothing failed so far in the job), <code>failure</code> (run only after a failure), or <code>always</code>.</td></tr>
        <tr><td><code>steps[].continueOnError</code></td><td>When true, a failure of this step does not mark the job as failed.</td></tr>
        </tbody>
    </table>
    <p>Globs use <code>*</code> to match within one path segment and <code>**</code> to match across segments. An omitted filter list matches everything. <code>toolpaths/*.json</code> matches <code>toolpaths/a.json</code> but not <code>toolpaths/x/a.json</code>; <code>toolpaths/**</code> matches both.</p>
  </section>

  <section id="step-reference" class="doc-section">
    <h2>Step reference</h2>
    <p>Pure steps read only the files of the commit being checked, never the live database, so a run reports on exactly the tree that was pushed. Service-backed steps are registered once their service exists. Until then, naming one is a hard error, not a silent no-op.</p>
    <table>
      <thead><tr><th>Step</th><th>with</th><th>Outputs</th><th>Fails when</th></tr></thead>
      <tbody>
        <tr><td><code>project/validate</code></td><td>none</td><td><code>issues</code> (0), <code>drawings</code>, <code>workflows</code></td><td>An entity JSON file is invalid, a <code>drawings/</code>, <code>components/</code>, or <code>toolpaths/</code> file's <code>id</code> does not match its file name, or <code>name</code> is missing.</td></tr>
        <tr><td><code>project/require-gcode</code></td><td>none</td><td><code>toolpaths</code></td><td>Any toolpath in the commit has no G-code.</td></tr>
        <tr><td><code>cam/verify</code></td><td><code>toolpath?</code>, <code>tolerance?</code>, <code>severity?</code> (<code>error</code> default, or <code>warning</code>)</td><td><code>verified</code>, <code>toolpaths</code>, <code>skipped</code></td><td>Replay finds an error (or a warning, with <code>severity: warning</code>): rapids into stock, gouges, shank or fixture collisions, material left. Programs without G-code or a stored generation request are listed in <code>skipped</code>, never counted as passing.</td></tr>
        <tr><td><code>cam/gcode</code></td><td><code>toolpath?</code></td><td><code>matched</code>, <code>toolpaths</code>, <code>skipped</code></td><td>Regenerating from the stored request does not reproduce the committed G-code (ignoring the header stamp and surrounding whitespace).</td></tr>
        <tr><td><code>cam/sequence</code></td><td><code>toolpath?</code></td><td><code>toolpaths</code>, <code>tools</code></td><td>One tool number names two different tools, or a rest-machining operation's <code>restToolDiameter</code> is not larger than its <code>toolDiameter</code>.</td></tr>
        <tr><td><code>vcs/commit</code></td><td><code>message</code> (required), <code>files</code> map</td><td><code>commit</code>, <code>files</code></td><td>The working copy is dirty, a path is outside <code>.cadcamfun/</code>, or a file is not <code>.yml</code>, <code>.yaml</code>, <code>.md</code>, <code>.json</code>, or <code>.txt</code>. An AI actor is refused on <code>main</code>.</td></tr>
        <tr><td><code>notify/push</code></td><td><code>title?</code>, <code>body?</code>, <code>outcome?</code> (<code>success</code> default, or <code>failure</code>)</td><td><code>notified</code></td><td>Never fails the run. Creates a <code>WORKFLOW_PASSED</code> or <code>WORKFLOW_FAILED</code> notification for the dispatching account, linked to the project history.</td></tr>
        <tr><td><code>ai/prompt</code></td><td><code>prompt</code> (required), <code>system?</code>, <code>model?</code></td><td><code>text</code>, <code>model</code></td><td>No model is available or the call fails. Only this step fails; the geometry verdict is unaffected.</td></tr>
        </tbody>
    </table>
    <p>Malformed <code>with</code> values are read defensively: a non-string where a string is expected, or a non-finite number, is ignored rather than failing the run. Check outputs to confirm a filter such as <code>toolpath</code> actually applied.</p>
  </section>

  <section id="interpolation" class="doc-section">
    <h2>Outputs and interpolation</h2>
    <p>A step with an <code>id</code> stores its outputs run-wide, so a job can read outputs of the jobs it <code>needs</code>. Inside any string in <code>with</code>, <code>$&#123;&#123; steps.&lt;id&gt;.outputs.&lt;key&gt; &#125;&#125;</code> is replaced recursively through objects and arrays. When a string consists of exactly one expression, the value keeps its type (a number stays a number). Embedded in other text, the value is converted to a string, and a missing value becomes an empty string. <code>ai/prompt</code> additionally expands <code>$&#123;&#123; files.&lt;path&gt; &#125;&#125;</code> to the text of that file in the commit (empty if the file is absent).</p>
    <table>
      <thead><tr><th>Expression in with</th><th>Output value</th><th>Result</th></tr></thead>
      <tbody>
        <tr><td><code>"$&#123;&#123; steps.verify.outputs.verified &#125;&#125;"</code></td><td><code>3</code></td><td><code>3</code> (number)</td></tr>
        <tr><td><code>"Verified $&#123;&#123; steps.verify.outputs.verified &#125;&#125; programs"</code></td><td><code>3</code></td><td><code>"Verified 3 programs"</code></td></tr>
        <tr><td><code>"$&#123;&#123; steps.missing.outputs.x &#125;&#125;"</code></td><td>none</td><td><code>undefined</code></td></tr>
        </tbody>
    </table>
  </section>

  <section id="run-lifecycle" class="doc-section">
    <h2>Run lifecycle and errors</h2>
    <ol>
      <li>The trigger event (push, pull request, or manual) selects workflows in the tree of the ref, read from the commit rather than the working copy.</li>
      <li>Each file is parsed. YAML errors (<code>yaml</code>), schema errors (<code>schema</code>), unknown <code>needs</code> (<code>needs</code>), cycles (<code>cycle</code>), and duplicate step IDs (<code>duplicate-step-id</code>) are reported as an invalid run with the reason.</li>
      <li>Every <code>uses</code> is checked against the registry before anything runs. An unknown step fails the run with <code>Unknown step(s): ...</code>, so a typo never half-applies a change.</li>
      <li>Jobs are ordered into levels. Each level runs concurrently; a job whose <code>needs</code> did not all succeed is skipped.</li>
      <li>Progress events (<code>job-start</code>, <code>step</code> with status and outputs or message, <code>job-end</code>) stream to the desktop and the companion, and the run is persisted with its events.</li>
      <li>The run succeeds only if every job succeeded. Skipped jobs count as not successful.</li>
    </ol>
    <p>Live events <code>workflow.started</code> (with the triggering event) and <code>workflow.finished</code> (<code>success</code>, <code>failure</code>, or <code>cancelled</code>) are published for each run.</p>
    <DocCallout type="note" title="Evidence, not decoration">
      <p>Of the built-in steps, only <code>cam/verify</code> replays the program against the stock and tools. The other checks confirm consistency of what was committed. Read <code>skipped</code> lists: an unverifiable toolpath is not a verified one.</p>
    </DocCallout>
  </section>

    <section id="engine-trace" class="doc-section">
    <h2>A full run, traced event by event</h2>
    <p>This workflow exercises every rule of the engine at once: outputs passed between jobs, a whole-value and an embedded interpolation, <code>continueOnError</code>, <code>if: failure</code>, a job skipped because its dependency failed, and an independent job. It was run with <code>runWorkflow</code> and four test step handlers: <code>demo/measure</code> returns <code>&#123; volume: 7842.92, part &#125;</code>, <code>demo/check</code> prints what it received, <code>demo/fail</code> fails with <code>StepError("tolerance exceeded")</code> and <code>demo/notify</code> returns nothing.</p>
    <DocCode language="yaml" title=".cadcamfun/workflows/release-check.yml">
{"name: Release check\non:\n  push: { branches: [main, \u0022release/**\u0022], paths: [\u0022parts/**/*.json\u0022] }\n  manual: {}\njobs:\n  measure:\n    steps:\n      - id: size\n        uses: demo/measure\n        with: { part: bracket }\n  verify:\n    needs: [measure]\n    steps:\n      - uses: demo/check\n        with: { volume: \u0022${{ steps.size.outputs.volume }}\u0022, label: \u0022vol=${{ steps.size.outputs.volume }} mm3\u0022 }\n      - uses: demo/fail\n        continueOnError: true\n      - uses: demo/fail\n      - uses: demo/notify\n        if: failure\n      - uses: demo/notify\n  publish:\n    needs: [verify]\n    steps:\n      - uses: demo/notify\n  lint:\n    steps:\n      - uses: demo/notify"}
    </DocCode>
    <DocCode language="text" title="Events and result, as emitted">
{"levels [[\u0022measure\u0022,\u0022lint\u0022],[\u0022verify\u0022],[\u0022publish\u0022]]\n{\u0022type\u0022:\u0022job-start\u0022,\u0022job\u0022:\u0022measure\u0022}\n{\u0022type\u0022:\u0022step\u0022,\u0022job\u0022:\u0022measure\u0022,\u0022index\u0022:0,\u0022uses\u0022:\u0022demo/measure\u0022,\u0022status\u0022:\u0022success\u0022,\u0022outputs\u0022:{\u0022volume\u0022:7842.92,\u0022part\u0022:\u0022bracket\u0022}}\n{\u0022type\u0022:\u0022job-end\u0022,\u0022job\u0022:\u0022measure\u0022,\u0022status\u0022:\u0022success\u0022}\n{\u0022type\u0022:\u0022job-start\u0022,\u0022job\u0022:\u0022lint\u0022}\n{\u0022type\u0022:\u0022step\u0022,\u0022job\u0022:\u0022lint\u0022,\u0022index\u0022:0,\u0022uses\u0022:\u0022demo/notify\u0022,\u0022status\u0022:\u0022success\u0022,\u0022outputs\u0022:{}}\n{\u0022type\u0022:\u0022job-end\u0022,\u0022job\u0022:\u0022lint\u0022,\u0022status\u0022:\u0022success\u0022}\n{\u0022type\u0022:\u0022job-start\u0022,\u0022job\u0022:\u0022verify\u0022}\n  check got {\u0022volume\u0022:7842.92,\u0022label\u0022:\u0022vol=7842.92 mm3\u0022}\n{\u0022type\u0022:\u0022step\u0022,\u0022job\u0022:\u0022verify\u0022,\u0022index\u0022:0,\u0022uses\u0022:\u0022demo/check\u0022,\u0022status\u0022:\u0022success\u0022,\u0022outputs\u0022:{\u0022ok\u0022:true}}\n{\u0022type\u0022:\u0022step\u0022,\u0022job\u0022:\u0022verify\u0022,\u0022index\u0022:1,\u0022uses\u0022:\u0022demo/fail\u0022,\u0022status\u0022:\u0022failure\u0022,\u0022message\u0022:\u0022Cause([Fail(StepError: tolerance exceeded)])\u0022}\n{\u0022type\u0022:\u0022step\u0022,\u0022job\u0022:\u0022verify\u0022,\u0022index\u0022:2,\u0022uses\u0022:\u0022demo/fail\u0022,\u0022status\u0022:\u0022failure\u0022,\u0022message\u0022:\u0022Cause([Fail(StepError: tolerance exceeded)])\u0022}\n{\u0022type\u0022:\u0022step\u0022,\u0022job\u0022:\u0022verify\u0022,\u0022index\u0022:3,\u0022uses\u0022:\u0022demo/notify\u0022,\u0022status\u0022:\u0022success\u0022,\u0022outputs\u0022:{}}\n{\u0022type\u0022:\u0022step\u0022,\u0022job\u0022:\u0022verify\u0022,\u0022index\u0022:4,\u0022uses\u0022:\u0022demo/notify\u0022,\u0022status\u0022:\u0022skipped\u0022}\n{\u0022type\u0022:\u0022job-end\u0022,\u0022job\u0022:\u0022verify\u0022,\u0022status\u0022:\u0022failure\u0022}\n{\u0022type\u0022:\u0022job-end\u0022,\u0022job\u0022:\u0022publish\u0022,\u0022status\u0022:\u0022skipped\u0022}\nresult {\u0022status\u0022:\u0022failure\u0022,\u0022jobs\u0022:{\u0022measure\u0022:\u0022success\u0022,\u0022lint\u0022:\u0022success\u0022,\u0022verify\u0022:\u0022failure\u0022,\u0022publish\u0022:\u0022skipped\u0022}}"}
    </DocCode>
    <table>
      <thead><tr><th>Observation</th><th>Rule</th></tr></thead>
      <tbody>
        <tr><td><code>measure</code> and <code>lint</code> run in the first level</td><td>Jobs without <code>needs</code> form level 0 and run concurrently</td></tr>
        <tr><td><code>volume</code> arrives as the number 7842.92, <code>label</code> as a string</td><td>A string that is exactly one expression keeps the output's type; an embedded expression is stringified</td></tr>
        <tr><td>Step 1 fails but the job continues normally</td><td><code>continueOnError: true</code> records the failure without marking the job failed</td></tr>
        <tr><td>Step 2 fails and marks the job failed</td><td>Default behaviour</td></tr>
        <tr><td>Step 3 runs, step 4 is skipped</td><td><code>if: failure</code> runs only after a failure; the default <code>if: success</code> is skipped after one</td></tr>
        <tr><td><code>publish</code> is skipped, never started</td><td>A job whose <code>needs</code> did not all succeed emits only <code>job-end</code> with <code>skipped</code></td></tr>
        <tr><td>Overall <code>failure</code></td><td>The run succeeds only if every job is <code>success</code>; a skipped job counts as not successful</td></tr>
        </tbody>
    </table>
  </section>

  <section id="trigger-matching" class="doc-section">
    <h2>Trigger matching, tested</h2>
    <table>
      <thead><tr><th>Event</th><th>Matches?</th><th>Why</th></tr></thead>
      <tbody>
        <tr><td>push to <code>main</code>, changed <code>parts/a/b.json</code></td><td>yes</td><td>Branch matches; path matches <code>parts/**/*.json</code></td></tr>
        <tr><td>push to <code>main</code>, changed <code>README.md</code></td><td>no</td><td>No changed path matches</td></tr>
        <tr><td>push to <code>release/6.1</code>, changed <code>parts/x.json</code></td><td>no</td><td><code>parts/**/*.json</code> compiles to <code>^parts\/.*\/[^/]*\.json$</code>, which needs a directory between <code>parts/</code> and the file</td></tr>
        <tr><td>push to <code>feature/x</code></td><td>no</td><td>Branch does not match</td></tr>
        <tr><td>manual</td><td>yes</td><td><code>manual</code> is declared</td></tr>
        <tr><td>pull request into <code>main</code></td><td>no</td><td>No <code>pullRequest</code> trigger declared</td></tr>
        </tbody>
    </table>
    <DocCallout type="warn" title="** needs a segment on each side here">
      <p>Because <code>**</code> becomes <code>.*</code> between two literal slashes, <code>parts/**/*.json</code> does not match files directly in <code>parts/</code>. List both patterns, <code>parts/*.json</code> and <code>parts/**/*.json</code>, to cover both.</p>
    </DocCallout>
    <code>*</code> matches within one path segment; <code>**</code> matches across segments. Omitting <code>branches</code> or <code>paths</code> means any value matches.
  </section>

  <section id="parse-errors" class="doc-section">
    <h2>Every parse error, reproduced</h2>
    <table>
      <thead><tr><th>code</th><th>Input problem</th><th>Message</th></tr></thead>
      <tbody>
        <tr><td><code>yaml</code></td><td>Malformed YAML such as <code>name: [x</code></td><td>Invalid YAML: SyntaxError: YAML Parse error: Unexpected token</td></tr>
        <tr><td><code>schema</code></td><td><code>uses: Shell</code> (not a lower-case <code>area/step</code> id)</td><td>SchemaError(Expected a string matching the RegExp …) with the path <code>["jobs"]["a"]["steps"][0]</code></td></tr>
        <tr><td><code>needs</code></td><td><code>needs: [b]</code> with no job <code>b</code></td><td>Job a needs unknown job b</td></tr>
        <tr><td><code>cycle</code></td><td><code>a</code> needs <code>b</code> and <code>b</code> needs <code>a</code></td><td>Workflow jobs form a cycle: a, b</td></tr>
        <tr><td><code>duplicate-step-id</code></td><td>Two steps with <code>id: s</code>, even in different jobs</td><td>Step ids must be unique across the workflow</td></tr>
        </tbody>
    </table>
    <p>At run time, any <code>uses</code> without a registered handler fails the whole run before the first step, listing every unknown id: <code>Unknown step(s): demo/measure, demo/check, …</code>. A typo therefore never half-applies a workflow.</p>
    <table>
      <thead><tr><th>Field</th><th>Rule</th></tr></thead>
      <tbody>
        <tr><td><code>name</code></td><td>1–128 characters</td></tr>
        <tr><td>Job and step ids</td><td>Letter first, then letters, digits, <code>_</code> or <code>-</code>; at most 64 characters</td></tr>
        <tr><td><code>uses</code></td><td>Lower-case segments separated by <code>/</code>, at least two segments</td></tr>
        <tr><td><code>steps</code></td><td>At least one per job</td></tr>
        <tr><td><code>if</code></td><td><code>success</code> (default), <code>always</code>, <code>failure</code></td></tr>
        </tbody>
    </table>
  </section>

